  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 110 PM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nIt will be warmer still for Monday as the upper high spreads \neastward and gets flattened on its northern extent by upper \ntroughing moving through the northern U.S.  H7 temperatures warm to \n+20 to +24 C which should put high temperatures well into the 100-\n107 degree range across much of the plains.  Based on climo and heat \nadvisory criteria, this resulted in some expansions to the ongoing \nHeat Advisories for Monday to include the lower Arkansas River \nvalley from Otero, Bent and Prowers counties.  It is possible later \nshifts may need to add Baca and Eastern Las Animas, but for the time \nbeing, wasn't getting widespread enough criteria for those areas. \nTemperatures over the San Luis Valley should climb into the mid 90s \nwhich is hot compared to climo for those areas as well.  With Heat \nAdvisory criteria being met, have added a Heat Advisory for the San \nLuis Valley as well.  Record Highs may come close or be challenged \nat many locations on Monday. \n\nRecord Highs for Aug 3rd: \n\nCOS: 99 in 1954\nPUB: 103 in 1902\nALS: 91 in 2024 \n\nA cold front moves through the southeast plains Monday night, \nsparking some isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. There \nare some timing differences with NAM12 on the earlier side of the \nspectrum.  This front may be accompanied with some strong north \nwinds gusting up to 40 mph given the tight surface pressure \ngradient.  Wind shift out of the east on Tuesday bringing low level \nmoisture return into the I-25 corridor and southeast mountains. \nMoisture appears to lag a bit but still sufficient to bring some \nhigher CAPE and increased shear for a strong storm or two. \nPrecipitable waters are around normal, or slightly above across the \nsoutheast plains, which is not excessive, but high enough to pose an \nenhanced risk for Flash flooding on burn scars across the southeast \nmountains.  Temperatures will drop back around 10-15 degrees across \nthe southeast plains and around 2-4 degrees out west, bringing a \nbreak from the heat.  \n\nIt will be slightly warmer on Wednesday with a more typical day 2 \nstrong to severe thunderstorm set up with easterly surface winds \nbeneath northwesterly flow aloft.  A disturbance moving through the \nflow will bring another round of thunderstorms in the afternoon \nwhich could be strong to severe for the southeast mountains and\nadjacent plains, depending on the low level moisture quality \nand resultant CAPE. This will be another elevated risk day for \nflash flooding on burn scars. \n\nAnother drying and warming trend sets in for late week as the  upper \nhigh builds again out west.  There are still some signals that \nmonsoon moisture will gradually return late next weekend or early \nnext week.  Until then, daily afternoon thunderstorm chances will \ncontinue over the mountains as weak waves move through west to\nnorthwest flow aloft. Until then, 100 degree temperatures will \nreturn for the plains for Friday into Saturday with 80s and \nlower 90s for the valleys, and 60s and 70s for the mountains. \n-KT",
